# Coach speech (Finnish): the building blocks every line uses (src/coach/catalog.cpp renders them).
# Same keys as assets/coach/speech/en/common.lang, plus the grammatical forms Finnish needs.
# Register: standard Finnish (yleiskieli), never colloquial forms ("sä", "mä", "sun", "ei oo"): the
# voice reads the words as written. The coach says "sinä" to the player and "minä" of himself, and
# drops subject pronouns as standard Finnish does ("Lyön ratsusi.", "Pelasit hyvin."). Finnish has no
# grammatical gender: nothing agrees with the coach's or the player's sex.
# Chess words as in the glossary (Finnish federation usage): sotilas, ratsu, lähetti, torni,
# kuningatar, kuningas; shakki, matti, patti, tasapeli, linnoitus, ohestalyönti, korotus.
# No value may be empty (tools/gen_opening_lang.py reads this file and refuses empty values).

# ---- Pieces --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nouns: sotilas, ratsu, lähetti, torni, kuningatar, kuningas (no gender). Finnish puts the case
# ending on the noun, then the possessive suffix: -si for "sinun" (your), -ni for "minun" (my). The
# suffix changes the stem ("kuningas" -> "kuninkaasi", illative "kuninkaaseen" -> "kuninkaaseesi"),
# so {your} and {my} are whole phrases: phrase.your.<type>.<form> and phrase.my.<type>.<form>.
# Default: the suffix alone, as standard Finnish writes it ("ratsusi", "lähettini"). It is always
# correct, and it is the only correct form when the owner is the subject of the clause ("Käytä
# {your:part}." = "Käytä torniasi.", "Siirrän {my:acc} ruutuun {sq}."; never "Käytä sinun torniasi").
# Forms, each defined for all six pieces and both owners (your knight / my bishop):
#   nom    ratsusi / lähettini         subject, predicate        "{your} on kiinnitetty.", "Tämä on {your}."
#   gen    ratsusi / lähettini         owner, postpositions      "{t1} on {your:gen} takana."
#   acc    ratsusi / lähettini         total object              "Lyön {your:acc}."
#          (with a suffix nom = gen = acc; the forms exist so that each sentence names its case)
#   part   ratsuasi / lähettiäni       partial object            "{my} uhkaa {your:part}.", "Siirsit {your:part} kahdesti."
#   ine    ratsussasi / lähetissäni    in                        "Nyt kaikki on kiinni {your:ine}."
#   ela    ratsustasi / lähetistäni    out of, about             "Pidä huolta {your:ela}.", "{your:ela} tulee kuningatar."
#   ill    ratsuusi / lähettiini       into, onto                "Hyökkäykseni kohdistuu {your:ill}."
#   ade    ratsullasi / lähetilläni    with (tool), "has"        "Lyö {my:acc} {your:ade}!", "{your:ade} oli parempi siirto."
#   abl    ratsultasi / lähetiltäni    from, "lacks"             "{your:abl} puuttuu pakoruutu."
#   all    ratsullesi / lähetilleni    to, for                   "Anna {your:all} pakoruutu."
#   ess    ratsunasi / lähettinäni     as (rare)                 "Se jatkaa pelissä {your:ess}."
#   tra    ratsuksesi / lähetikseni    becoming (rare)           "Tämä sotilas muuttuu {your:tra}."
# Every form also exists in two variants (all six pieces):
#   <form>_emph  (your / my) with the pronoun, to stress whose piece it is: "sinun ratsusi", "minun
#                lähettiäni" (part_emph): "Tämä on {my:nom_emph}, ei {your:nom_emph}." Never when the
#                owner is the subject of the clause, and not as a habit: the default is the natural form.
#   bare_<form>  (every owner) the noun alone: "ratsu", "ratsun", "ratsua"... For the kind of piece
#                rather than this piece: "{your:bare_nom} on vahvin nappula.", "Sotilas korottuu
#                {your:bare_tra}." (= "kuningattareksi").
# The object: after an imperative, a passive or "täytyy", Finnish puts a total object in the
# nominative ("Lyö ratsu!", "Ratsu lyödään."). With a suffix nothing changes; with bare_* and with
# white / black use nom there and acc elsewhere ("{reply} lyö {piece:acc}" = "valkean ratsun").
# Pronouns: Finnish says "se" (it) for every piece, so no form is needed: write se / sen / sitä /
# siinä / siitä / siihen / sillä / siltä / sille in the sentence itself ("{your} on uhattuna. Siirrä
# sitä!", "{my} lyö sen.", "Ota se!" after an imperative). "Its" is "sen" ("sen takana", "sen ruutu").
# Nouns in every case without a possessive (used by white / black and bare_*).
